The 2026 MTBO season has now drawn to a close. It was a truly special and successful year for Swedish mountain bike orienteering. Although we had fewer major national events than in some previous years, that was primarily because we hosted the World Championships on home soil. Sweden truly flew the MTBO flag high on the world stage in 2026, and the entire season served as a steady progression that gained momentum with every passing month.

## Spring: Haninge Kicked Off the Cup

The domestic competitive season got underway in earnest in May when Haninge SOK hosted the Swedish Cup at Rudan. Classic Södertörn terrain with both sprint and long-distance races provided an early benchmark for winter training and set the tone for this year's cup.

## Summer: O-Ringen in Gothenburg

In July, the orienteering community gathered in Gothenburg for O-Ringen. A sizeable field assembled with many international competitors taking part. Notably, the Swiss national team travelled to Gothenburg to race in Swedish competitions and build up towards the World Championships.

The West Coast’s undulating terrain and rocky singletracks differed completely from the fast pine-heath terrain awaiting later in Dalarna, but the visiting cyclists found it well worthwhile to be in Sweden and build race pace in their legs.

## The Swedish Championships Weekend in Uppsala

In mid-August, IF Thor and MTBO Uppland hosted the year's Swedish Championships. We tested a slightly different concept with two races on Saturday followed by one on Sunday:

- **Saturday morning:** Middle distance at OK Linné’s club house, demanding split-second decisions across an intricate trail network.
- **Saturday afternoon:** Sprint racing in a distinctive university and park setting across SLU and Ultuna.
- **Sunday:** A demanding long-distance test in Nåsten where physical stamina and pure route choices proved decisive.

Coming just over a week before the World Championships, the weekend attracted several top international riders from France, Czechia, Austria, and Japan looking to fine-tune their form in Sweden. This gave the weekend an extra international dimension and generated tough competition in the forest.

## World Championships and Junior World Championships in Mora

The season's absolute highlight was the World Championships and Junior World Championships in Mora in late August. The competitions were staged in prime pine-heath terrain around Arena Hemus and Moraparken, supported by top-tier maps.

![Klaus Csúcs from IFK Mora OK and the organising team reviewing GPS tracking with a competitor at Arena Hemus](~/assets/images/posts/mtbo-sasongen-2026-sammanfattning/arrangorsgruppen-klaus-csucs.jpg)

The organising team, led by Klaus Csúcs, invested tremendous effort over an extended period to provide every participant with the best possible racing conditions. Delivering a World Championship requires vast commitment from volunteers and clubs, and the delivery in Mora showcased Swedish event organisation at its finest.

Opinions varied among competitors regarding the technical difficulty of the courses. Some found the navigation straightforward to read, while others found it remarkably intricate. In practice, much depended on physical condition and how fast riders could sustain their pace. At flat-out speed, intense concentration was essential at every track junction to avoid errors, demanding a continuous balance between pressing at full throttle or keeping a steady hand on the reins and easing the pace slightly to maintain full navigational control.

Alongside the championship classes, three days of open public races took place on the very same maps. This gave recreational riders and supporters the opportunity to cycle through the same quality terrain and contributed to a great atmosphere during the final days of racing.

## Swedish Success and Medals

The Swedish team produced outstanding performances throughout the championship week in Mora:

- **Gabriella Gustafsson** delivered a historic championship performance on home ground, securing four World Championship medals in the women's elite category: double individual World Champion titles in the Middle and Long distance, Silver in the Sprint, and Silver in the Women's Senior Relay.
- **The Women's Relay Team** with Tilda Palm, Elvira Larsson, and Gabriella Gustafsson took a hard-fought World Championship Silver.
- **Joel Holmgård** thoroughly dominated the junior ranks (JWMTBOC), executing a clean sweep with triple World Championship gold medals: victories in the Sprint, Middle, and Long distance.
- **Signe Feil** earned medals across every single individual discipline in JWMTBOC, capturing Silver in the Sprint, Silver in the Middle distance, and Bronze in the Long distance.

![Map excerpt and GPS tracking from the World Championship Relay in Mora via Livelox where forking and a dense track network demanded full control at high speed](~/assets/images/posts/mtbo-sasongen-2026-sammanfattning/wmtboc26-stafett-karta-livelox.jpg)

![Rasmus Nordgren (OK Kåre) punching a control during the World Championships in Mora](~/assets/images/posts/mtbo-sasongen-2026-sammanfattning/rasmus-nordgren-stampling-hemus.jpg)

Behind the medals, strong performances were achieved across all age categories, from youth and juniors through to masters competitors.

